The kernel collection's segments that XNU hands back once used go to the VM (kc-segment-release, default 1).

kern.boot_payload_memory of the last desktop boots reads "freed 0 pages, dropped 5 calls 0x2810000 bytes": every
ml_static_mfree() request for memory of the loaded kernel collection was dropped, 40 MiB that stay wired (and counted
in VM_KERN_COUNT_WIRED_STATIC_KERNELCACHE) for the session. The requests are the collection's __LINKEDIT
(OSKext::jettisonFileSetLinkeditSegment, 0x272c000, made unless keepsyms is set), the kernel's __BOOTDATA
(arm_vm_prot_finalize after the boot stacks are replaced, 0xd8000), __KLDDATA (0x8000) and __KLD (0x4000)
(OSKext::removeKextBootstrap). On a kernel without a monitor, without the SPTM and without ARM_LARGE_MEMORY the
physical aperture aliases only the memory in front of the collection and behind topOfKernelData, so the static
address of these segments lies below physmap_base and ml_static_mfree() returns at "It is acceptable (if bad) to fail
to free". A monitor kernel slides the same segments into the aperture so that they can be freed, and after
the free arm_vm_prot_finalize() clears their static mappings there: nothing of XNU reads them once given back.

arm_vm_prot_init() maps each of these segments with page mappings and no contiguous hint (granule 0). ml_static_protect()
and ml_static_mfree() now take a range that lies wholly inside one of them by its static address, as they take a
range of the image tail (payload-addon-release.patch): every page gets the protections of free memory and keeps its
static address as its kernel address (phystokv() of a collection page is that address). Pages before the page array
are found through the radix tree whose root payload-radix-root-at-startup.patch makes during startup. Ranges in any
other segment of the collection are dropped as before. kc-segment-release=0 restores the old behaviour.

kern.boot_payload_memory shows the result: about 10256 more pages freed and no bytes dropped for these segments.

Has not run on hardware.

